   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the multiple dwelling law, in relation to interim multiple\ndwellings",
    "basePrintNo": "S6950",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6950-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §282-a, Mult Dwell L",
    "lawSection": "Multiple Dwelling Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6950\n\nSPONSOR: SALAZAR\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the multiple dwelling law, in relation to interim multi-\nple dwellings\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill makes it unlawful for any loft owner to interrupt, deny, or\ndiscontinue essential services, or impair the habitability of an interim\nmultiple dwelling unit or building for tenants covered by the loft law.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one adds a new paragraph to section 282-a of the multiple dwell-\ning law, making it unlawful for any owner to interrupt, deny, or discon-\ntinue essential services, or impair the habitability of an interim\nmultiple dwelling unit or building.\n\nSection two sets an immediate effective date and shall apply to any\naction or proceeding pending or on appeal on or after such date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMultiple dwelling law section 282-a protects tenants with pending appli-\ncations for loft law coverage from owners denying essential services in\nan attempt to pressure tenants from dropping their application for\ncoverage. Recently, the specific focus of MDL 282-a has been used to\nargue that tenants covered by the loft law may not bring HP cases\nagainst owners, and must instead be dealt with by the Loft Board as set\nforth in the Board's housing maintenance regulations.  A HP case is an\naffirmative legal action a tenant can bring in NYC Housing Court to\nforce the landlord to make repairs, or to correct a lack of heat or hot\nwater or other services or emergency conditions or to correct building\nviolations.\n\nThe Loft Board's unique focus is the conversion of former commercial or\nmanufacturing spaces to safe, lawful residential units. Being provided\nessential services and a habitable living space are universal housing\nneeds and are in no way unique to lofts or loft tenants. This bill will\nensure that covered loft tenants can bring HP cases in housing court\nwhen essential services are denied, or habitability is impaired.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of November next succeeding the\ndate on which it shall have become law.\n",
